Context: this is the last page of the chapter on the apparent diameters and the true hourly motion of the Sun and Moon in lunar eclipses. On the previous page Euler had turned the lunar longitude series into a trigonometric series for the Moon's hourly motion. He then calibrated its constant against a direct two-epoch computation from his tables (1774.7" against 1775.7", so the constant was lowered by 1"). He also gave special formulas for conjunction (η=0) and opposition (η=180°), where v is the Moon's eccentric anomaly reckoned from apogee, V is the Sun's anomaly, and η is the elongation. Those formulas are 33'40.8" (conjunction) or 33'43.1" (opposition) − 258.3 cos v + 11.7 cos 2v − 1.8 cos V + 1.4 cos(v−V).

Top of this page (end of §14). He evaluates the special formulas with the Moon at perigee (v=180°). With the Sun at apogee (V=0): 2290.8 − 3.2 = 38'7.6" in conjunction and 38'9.9" in opposition. With the Sun at perigee: 38'14.0" and 38'16.3". He adds that when the Moon is at apogee the solar effect, ±0.4", is under half a second.

§15. The full corrected series for the hourly motion, in arcseconds, is
1982.0 − 216.4 cos v + 8.8 cos 2v + 0.5 cos V − 0.7 cos(v+V) + 0.7 cos(v−V) − 1.1 cos η + 39.9 cos 2η + 3.0 cos(2η−2v) − 0.6 cos(2η+v) − 41.1 cos(2η−v) − 1.0 cos(2η+V) − 1.3 cos(2η−V) + 0.7 cos(2η−v+V) + 0.4 cos(2η−v−V).
Under each coefficient he writes its common logarithm, for computing. In modern terms the series is the time-derivative of the lunar longitude series:
- the cos v term is the derivative of the equation of centre;
- cos 2η is the variation;
- cos(2η−v) is the evection;
- cos 2v is the second-order equation of centre;
- the V terms are the annual-equation and parallactic-type couplings with the solar anomaly.
He proposes adjoining to the lunar tables a dedicated hourly-motion table. It would be given to tenths of a second so that accumulated rounding stays under 1", and arranged in twelve parts like the motion table. It is meant for eclipse and occultation work.

§16. Setting η=90° or 270° gives the quadrature formula 1942.1 − 174.7 cos v + 5.8 cos 2v + 2.8 cos V − 1.1 cos(v+V). The terms combine as follows:
- 1982.0 − 39.9 = 1942.1;
- −216.4 + 0.6 + 41.1 = −174.7;
- 8.8 − 3.0 = 5.8;
- 0.5 + 1.0 + 1.3 = 2.8;
- −0.7 − 0.4 = −1.1 for cos(v+V), while the two cos(v−V) contributions cancel.
At the Moon's apogee this gives 1773.2 + 1.7 cos V, i.e. 1775" (Sun at apogee) or 1771" (Sun at perigee). At the Moon's perigee it gives 2122.6 + 3.9 cos V, i.e. 2126" or 2119".
He concludes that the hourly motion is least at quadrature with the Moon at apogee and the Sun at perigee (about 29'31"). It is greatest at opposition with both bodies at perigee (38'16.3").
